<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The mother-in-law, about 61 and suffering from spinal ailments, owned a house in Jodhpur purchased by registered sale deed in 1998 — before the petitioner\'s marriage into the family. In February 2020 the Maintenance Tribunal ordered the daughter-in-law and her husband to vacate within a month, and directed the husband to pay his mother ₹4,000 per month. The daughter-in-law obtained a stay from the High Court within weeks, and the matter remained in limbo for six years. She argued collusion between mother and son to dispossess her, claimed the house was a shared household bought by her late father-in-law, and pointed to its separate entrances.</p>

<h2>How the court resolved the statutory collision</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>The daughter-in-law\'s frame</h3>
    <p>Shared household under the 2005 Act; residence right protected by the Supreme Court\'s shared-household jurisprudence; eviction a device in a matrimonial dispute.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>The court\'s frame</h3>
    <p>Self-acquired property of a senior citizen; no proprietary right shown by the petitioner; harmonious reading of the two Acts; a matrimonial dispute with the husband cannot by itself deny the mother statutory protection in her own home.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Three strands in the reasoning</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ownership first:</strong> the petitioner established no independent proprietary right. Residence after marriage is not ownership, and the claim that the father-in-law had bought the house remained unproved against the registered deed.</li>
  <li><strong>Harmony, not hierarchy:</strong> the court acknowledged the residence protections of the 2005 Act but held that a woman\'s protection does not require a senior citizen to remain in an unsafe or detrimental environment. Each case turns on its facts — the eviction power exists, and its exercise is discretionary.</li>
  <li><strong>Recent Supreme Court confirmation:</strong> the court relied on the Supreme Court\'s 2026 clarification that the tribunal may order eviction to ensure the senior citizen\'s maintenance and protection, quoting that eviction is "an incident of enforcement of right to maintenance and protection."</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>What this means for both sides of these disputes</h2>
<p>For senior citizens, the path that succeeded here is instructive: a documented, self-acquired title; a tribunal application framed around protection rather than the family quarrel; and patience through the writ challenge. For daughters-in-law, the judgment underlines where the real security lies — the DV Act remedies against the husband, including a residence order or alternative accommodation under Section 19, survive an eviction under the 2007 Act and should be pursued in parallel rather than held back as a defence. An eviction order against the couple does not decide maintenance, stridhan or custody questions, all of which continue before their own forums.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Six years of interim stay dissolved in a single hearing once the title documents and the Supreme Court\'s 2026 ruling were placed side by side. Interim protection in writ proceedings is not an asset to be banked on; parties resisting eviction should use the stay period to secure their position under the DV Act, not merely to prolong occupation.</p>
</div>
